# gamingpc-xubuntu.env — the per-node DATA the bare-metal procedure consumes for
# the fleet's FOURTH k3s pool node: the maintainer's gaming PC, a dual-boot
# Windows/Xubuntu desktop on the home LAN.
#
# THIS FILE CARRIES NO PROCEDURE. It is read by the same ../profile.sh parser,
# the same ../../provision-k3s.sh and the same ansible/ci-pool.yml roles as
# the other three profiles. A fourth pool node is a fourth PROFILE, never a
# fourth procedure (SPECIFICATION/non-functional-requirements.md §"Runner-pool
# node rebuild recipe", "One profile per node"). Read ./poweredge-xubuntu.env's
# header for the format.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE. Maintainer direction to plan homelab-k8s-worker-nodes
# (epic livespec-dev-tooling-ko26ez), 2026-11-11 ~00:40Z: "gamingpc-xubuntu-bootstrap
# has the new box provisioned. You need to get a node running on it asap as
# part of your plan". The seat chose a bare-metal k3s AGENT (this profile)
# rather than a Lima container worker, because the host is native Linux amd64
# on wired Ethernet; the shape is hp-xubuntu's (Windows drive protected,
# Xubuntu on its own drive, runner-only CI with no storage tiers). The host
# record is the private repository thewoolleyman/gamingpc-xubuntu-info
# (README.md and docs/hardware.md at dc83a73); its bootstrap is plan
# gamingpc-xubuntu-bootstrap, epic livespec-dev-tooling-p37yia.
#
# STAGED ADMISSION, AND THIS IS STAGE TWO: ENROLLED IN CI, GATES AND FACTORY.
# Stage one (merged first) joined the node closed to every class, because any
# ci_converge_unit applies committed queue capacity cluster-wide and a node
# that did not exist yet must not be promised work (legacy-coordinator-agent
# ordering check, 2026-11-11). This stage lands only once the node reads Ready,
# and opens it with the split below in one change: runner-only CI with its
# churn slot and local-path entry, the gate lane, FACTORY_NODE=true with its
# factory budget, the cohort quota (31) and the dev-tooling scale-set ceiling,
# and the node's ci_pool membership and host_vars.
#
# DEFAULT ENROLLMENT, DERIVED FROM ITS RESOURCES (the 2026-11-07 maintainer
# order that every new node arrives enrolled with a sensible default split of
# CI, gate and factory load; coordinator-duties §10). The split reuses
# homelab-worker/enroll-workload-classes.sh's rule: allocatable CPU divided by
# the three enrolled classes, a churn slot at about 2.73 CPUs
# (../../phase2/kueue/DERIVATION.md), at least one unit per class.
#   * allocatable, EXPECTED with the KUBELET_ARGS below: 16 threads (nproc 16,
#     measured 2026-11-11 ~02:05Z) - 1.5 CPU reserved = 14500m; 30 GiB memory
#     total (same read) - 3 GiB reserved - 0.5 GiB eviction floor = about
#     26.5 GiB. To be confirmed by `kubectl describe node` at join.
#   * one third each = 4833m CPU per class;
#   * CI: ADMISSION_CAPACITY_C=1 (4833m / 2730m, rounded down, at least 1);
#   * gates: one lane of 5 cpu / 8 Gi in gates-cq (gates-gamingpc-flavor). A
#     gate requests 5 CPU / 6 GiB (DERIVATION.md "The hp numbers"), so the
#     "at least one unit" floor lifts the 4833m share to one gate;
#   * factory: factory_pod_budget=1 (ansible host_vars). A factory run is
#     4 cpu / 8 Gi (../../phase2/factory/DERIVATION.md); one third of memory is
#     about 8.8 GiB, so MEMORY binds first at one run.
#   Committed CPU at full load: 2.73 + 5 + 4 = 11.73 of 14.5. A first value,
#   to be revised from measured runs per hour, green rate and CI p95, as every
#   node's numbers have been.
#
# DUAL BOOT IS A STANDING DEPARTURE. Windows Boot Manager stays FIRST in the
# boot order (the bootstrap's protected-install condition), so every reboot
# leaves this node absent until the maintainer picks the Xubuntu entry (M2_2)
# at the F11 one-time boot menu, and any Windows session is a departure.
# Work placed here must survive that (plan homelab-k8s-worker-nodes,
# ko26ez.8's subject).
NODE_NAME=gamingpc-xubuntu

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RAID controller — none. Two NVMe drives on the motherboard's M.2 slots.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONTROLLER_KIND=none
CONTROLLER_CLI=
CONTROLLER_ID=
VD_ENCLOSURE=
VD_SLOTS=
VD_RAID_LEVEL=
VD_STRIP_KIB=
VD_CACHE_POLICY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Disks — EVERYTHING IS PRESERVED, and THE BASE-OS STAGE IS NOT A STAGE THIS
# NODE RUNS. Xubuntu 26.04 is already installed (bootstrap, 2026-11-10/11):
# a 1 GiB FAT32 ESP and an ext4 root on the Xubuntu Crucial P310, and the
# WINDOWS installation on the OTHER Crucial P310, which must never be touched
# (the maintainer's install approval was conditional on Windows boot and
# operation remaining intact).
#
# Both drives are the same model and size. Measured read-only on the installed
# system 2026-11-11 ~02:05Z (relayed by legacy-coordinator-agent): nvme0n1 is
# Xubuntu (p1 1G vfat /boot/efi, p2 1.8T ext4 /), nvme1n1 is Windows (p1 200M
# vfat ESP, p2 16M MSR, p3 1.8T NTFS, p4 845M NTFS recovery), all unmounted.
# NVMe ENUMERATION CAN SWAP across boots, so these NAMES are not identities.
# The stable identity is /dev/disk/by-id/nvme-<model>_<serial>, but the host
# record deliberately keeps the drives' full serials OUT of git (private
# operator evidence only), so by-id paths are not committed here. The names are
# harmless only because no stage that reads them runs on this node:
# CI_NODE_FACILITIES=runner-only plans no storage, and the base-OS stage is
# never run. Re-identify both drives by serial before ANY stage that writes a
# disk. The WHOLE Windows drive is preserved, and both Xubuntu partitions.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DISK_PLAN=free-space
PRESERVED_PARTITIONS=/dev/nvme0n1p1 /dev/nvme0n1p2 /dev/nvme1n1
TARGET_DEVICE=/dev/nvme0n1
ESP_DEVICE=/dev/nvme0n1p1
ESP_SIZE=1GiB
ESP_FSTYPE=vfat
ESP_LABEL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI posture — RUNNER-ONLY, as hp-xubuntu. No storage tiers: runner work
# volumes live under k3s's own default local-path directory on the ext4 root
# (2 TB NVMe). It is the same path as the provisioner's
# DEFAULT_PATH_FOR_NON_LISTED_NODES, and the node is still listed explicitly in
# ../../phase2/local-path-provisioner/local-path-provisioner.yaml's nodePathMap,
# as every runner-only node is.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CI_NODE_FACILITIES=runner-only
RUNNER_WORKVOL_DIR=/var/lib/rancher/k3s/storage
PV_PARTLABEL=
VOLUME_GROUPS=
LOGICAL_VOLUMES=
ROLE_TIERS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base operating system — the installed system's facts, read by the bootstrap
# (kernel 7.0.0-43-generic). The base-OS stage never runs here; the mirror,
# component, kernel-package and initramfs keys are UNVERIFIED (copied from the
# fleet's other resolute nodes) and are read by nothing on this node.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
OS_DISTRIBUTION=ubuntu
OS_RELEASE=resolute
OS_ARCHITECTURE=amd64
# UNVERIFIED
OS_MIRROR=http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
# UNVERIFIED
OS_SECURITY_MIRROR=http://security.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
# UNVERIFIED
OS_COMPONENTS=main restricted universe multiverse
# UNVERIFIED
KERNEL_PACKAGE=linux-image-generic
# UNVERIFIED
INITRAMFS_GENERATOR=dracut
ROOT_LABEL=
SWAP_LABEL=
# UNVERIFIED
BOOT_ENTRY_LABEL=Ubuntu
# The installed operator account (key-only SSH, NOPASSWD sudo), measured by
# the bootstrap; the fleet's other nodes use cwoolley.
OPERATOR_ACCOUNT=ubuntu
OPERATOR_GROUPS=sudo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network — the wired Realtek RTL8126 (r8169) on enp10s0, negotiated at 1 Gbps.
# The board also carries Wi-Fi 7 (ath12k); if it ever joins the same subnet,
# the node IP must be the WIRED address, which is why it is pinned.
#
# NODE_ADDRESS measured on the running Xubuntu 2026-11-11 ~02:05Z (relayed by
# legacy-coordinator-agent): enp10s0 192.168.1.179/24, default route via
# 192.168.1.1 on enp10s0, DHCP (the maintainer reserved this adapter's address
# on 2026-11-10). On the tailnet since 02:01:06Z as 100.80.153.83.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_NETWORK_INTERFACE=enp10s0
NODE_ADDRESS=192.168.1.179/24

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Factory posture and kubelet budget. FACTORY_NODE=true: the node carries the
# ci-runner.io/factory label (node_labels reconciles it, since k3s applies
# --node-label only at registration and stage one registered without it), and
# ansible/ci-pool.yml's factory_budget_reconciler holds it to its host_vars
# factory_pod_budget (1, see the header). Sandbox pods only: no Fabro server and
# no fabro-hosts inventory role.
#
# KUBELET_ARGS mirror hp-xubuntu's reserves for the same 16-thread class
# (1.5 CPU and 3 GiB reserved, 512 MiB eviction floor), so the system and the
# desktop session keep headroom beside the pods. NODE_DATA_DIR stays the k3s
# default on the root NVMe.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FACTORY_NODE=true
NODE_DATA_DIR=
KUBELET_ARGS=system-reserved=cpu=1000m,memory=2048Mi kube-reserved=cpu=500m,memory=1024Mi eviction-hard=memory.available<512Mi,nodefs.available<10%,imagefs.available<10%

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cluster role — an AGENT joining the same server as the other agents. The
# join token is read at run time from CLUSTER_TOKEN_FILE (mode 0600, root);
# this tree carries no secret.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLUSTER_ROLE=agent
CLUSTER_JOIN_ADDRESS=https://192.168.1.200:6443
CLUSTER_TOKEN_FILE=/etc/rancher/k3s/agent-join-token
# Open to CI scheduling (stage one's node-role/ci=joining taint is removed by
# the node_taints role on apply).
NODE_TAINTS=
CHURN_KUBECONFIG_FILE=/etc/rancher/k3s/node-status-kubeconfig
ADMISSION_CAPACITY_C=1
